{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LiPLsUF7HbMAJF4AS4ziAB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tENHANCE SESSION WORKFLOW (GAP-FOCUSED RESEARCH OVER AUDITED SESSIONS)\n2\t\n3\tYou will run a single enhance session that reads audited session outputs and fills gaps using new, audited content. The source sessions may come from any audited skill (research, review, or a prior enhance). Each source contains per-paragraph audit footnotes with Confidence levels (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW).\n4\t\n5\tInputs (absolute paths provided):\n6\t- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}                     # Objectives / target state\n7\t- KB_DIR (optional): {{KB_DIR}}                # Optional KB context (markdown docs)\n8\t- SESSIONS_DIR: {{SESSIONS_DIR}}               # Root directory with prior workflow sessions\n9\t- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}                 # Current enhance session directory\n10\t- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}\n11\t- CITATIONS_RULES: {{CITATIONS_RULES_PATH}}    # Typically citations.enhance.md\n12\t- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}            # Paragraph-level audit rules\n13\t- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json      # CLI-provided manifest (authoritative I/O)\n14\t\n15\tData sources (local only):\n16\t- The SOURCES listed in the objective are the audited session files to enhance.\n17\t  Read each source file. These contain text with per-paragraph audit footnotes.\n18\t- MANIFEST is authoritative for the output filename (`outputs.primary`).\n19\t- Optional KB markdown files in KB_DIR for additional context (never as sole evidence).\n20\t\n21\tGoal:\n22\tProduce ONE session-level enhance document, fully audited paragraph-by-paragraph.\n23\tOutput filename is controlled by MANIFEST:\n24\t- Read MANIFEST first.\n25\t- Write the session output to:\n26\t  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n27\tDo NOT derive output filenames from the workflow filename or from TASK_FILE.\n28\t\n29\tThis document:\n30\t- May introduce new or corrected content to close gaps in the audited KB.\n31\t- MUST attach an audit footnote to every paragraph according to AUDIT_RULES.\n32\t- MUST keep citations in TOKEN form ([KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]), not [@Key].\n33\t- WILL LATER be consumed by a compile-type workflow (compile-enhance) that converts tokens to [@Key] and generates a bibliography file.\n34\t\n35\tMethod:\n36\t\n37\t1) Load objectives from TASK_FILE\n38\t   - Read TASK_FILE and extract:\n39\t     - Overall objectives and required deliverables.\n40\t     - Explicit focus areas, questions, and constraints.\n41\t   - Treat TASK_FILE as the **target state** you want the session output document to satisfy.\n42\t   - If TASK_FILE contains `## SLOTS` and `## CONSTRAINTS`, treat it as a\n43\t     structured prompt. Parse the slot headings and treat each slot as a\n44\t     separate section in the output. For each slot, follow that slot's\n45\t     instructions including any format directive in the description.\n46\t\n47\t2) Read MANIFEST and audited source sessions\n48\t- Read MANIFEST from {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json to obtain `outputs.primary`.\n49\t- Read each file listed in SOURCES (from the objective). These are audited\n50\t  session documents containing text with per-paragraph audit footnotes.\n51\t- For each paragraph across the source sessions:\n52\t     - Read its existing audit footnote (`^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]`).\n53\t       - If the rationale states that the KB paragraph is incorrect, contradicted by evidence, or contains serious factual errors, or if `Confidence` is LOW → **requires correction**.\n54\t       - If the rationale highlights gaps, mixed evidence, or unresolved doubts, or if `Confidence` is MEDIUM  **requires clarification / additional evidence**.\n55\t       - If the rationale states that the KB paragraph is well supported and `Confidence` is HIGH  factually sound; candidate to preserve, but may still be incomplete w.r.t. objectives.\n56\t   - Cross-check against TASK_FILE objectives:\n57\t     - Identify which objectives are fully covered by HIGH-confidence paragraphs whose rationales indicate strong support.\n58\t     - Identify objectives partially covered (need expansion or detail).\n59\t     - Identify objectives not covered at all.\n60\t\n61\t3) Targeted research for gaps\n62\t   - For each gap (LOW/MEDIUM confidence, rationales indicating contradictions/uncertainty, partially covered objectives, or missing objectives):\n63\t     - Use web search/fetch to gather **authoritative external evidence**.\n64\t       - Prefer DOIs / ARXIV / official standards / institutional reports.\n65\t     - DO NOT rely solely on low-confidence or contradicted/uncertain paragraphs as evidence.\n66\t     - For HIGH-confidence paragraphs, you MAY use their content as KB context, but any new claims must be backed by explicit external citations.\n67\t   - Summarize the relevant evidence for each gap, keeping notes of which sources (WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/KB:) support which claims.\n68\t\n69\t4) Draft new and improved paragraphs (with citations)\n70\t   - For each gap:\n71\t     - Write one or more new or revised paragraphs that:\n72\t       - Correct FALSE statements.\n73\t       - Clarify or strengthen UNCERTAIN or low-confidence claims.\n74\t       - Add missing content for uncovered objectives.\n75\t     - Attach inline TOKEN citations to support each factual claim:\n76\t       - `[WEB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]`, and, when appropriate, `[KB:...]`.\n77\t     - Maintain a professional technical tone and clear structure per FORMAT_RULES.\n78\t   - Preserve TRUE/HIGH paragraphs from source sessions when they already satisfy objectives:\n79\t     - You may copy them verbatim or lightly edit for clarity, but DO NOT change their meaning.\n80\t     - If you expand them, ensure new statements have explicit external citations.\n81\t\n82\t5) Apply per-paragraph audit (AUDIT_RULES)\n83\t   - For **every paragraph** in the session document (preserved, corrected, expanded, or new):\n84\t     - Apply AUDIT_RULES to produce:\n85\t       - `Confidence: HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW` about how well supported the paragraph is by the available evidence.\n86\t       - A `Rationale` summarizing the evidence and reasoning, explicitly stating whether the paragraph is well supported, incorrect, or uncertain and why.\n87\t     - Append the audit footnote immediately after the paragraph in the canonical format:\n88\t       - `^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]`\n89\t   - This step is mandatory: no paragraph may remain without an audit footnote.\n90\t\n91\t6) Post-processing and citation rules\n92\t   - Apply FORMAT_RULES to the full session document:\n93\t     - Professional voice, math notation, heading levels, etc.\n94\t   - Apply CITATIONS_RULES for **session-style** citations:\n95\t     - Normalize all citations to TOKEN form: `[KB:...]`, `[WEB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]`.\n96\t     - You MUST NOT convert citations to `[@Key]` in this workflow.\n97\t\n98\t7) Write session output\n99\t   - Read MANIFEST and use `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n100\t   - Save/write the full audited session document to:\n101\t     - {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n102\t   - Confirm in your final message (without printing document contents):\n103\t     - \"Enhanced session document written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n104\t\n105\tConstraints:\n106\t- Use only local audit outputs and optional KB files as starting context; **all new or corrected claims must be supported by explicit external citations** via web search/fetch.\n107\t- Do NOT perform any compile/consensus logic across multiple enhance sessions; each run is an independent session.\n108\t- Keep citations in TOKEN form; do not emit `[@Key]`.\n109\t- If you include Markdown code blocks in the output, treat them as opaque: do not place citations or audit footnotes inside the block; place any required audit footnote immediately after the closing fence.\n110\t- Do not create any separate bibliography file in this workflow (no `biblatex.bib` here).\n111\t- Do not treat this workflow's output as a client-ready deliverable; it is an intermediate, audited session document to be consumed by `tito compile`.\n112\t\n113\tEnhancement Principles:\n114\t- Contradicted/incorrect claims (per rationale) → Correct with authoritative sources\n115\t- Uncertain or LOW/MEDIUM confidence claims → Resolve with additional evidence\n116\t- Missing objectives → Research and add new content\n117\t- Incomplete coverage → Expand with proper detail\n118\t- All enhancements → Must have citation support\n119\t- Preserve HIGH-confidence content → Only expand if objectives require it\n120\t\n121\tExternal capabilities (if available):\n122\t- Web access (search + fetch): REQUIRED for gap research and fact verification\n123\t- File reading: to read the audited source sessions listed in SOURCES\n124\t- Optional orchestrator memory tool: MAY be used strictly as shared cache/state for repeated web work. Do NOT cite memory as evidence; all new or corrected claims must still be supported by explicit external citations.\n125\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"249e2e93-5ddb-4b1b-a788-8df9e70b5896","uuid":"57739392-4c37-499e-a46c-bae8c0aa5cc6","timestamp":"2026-03-30T21:26:51.545Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/workflows/enhance.session.md","content":"ENHANCE SESSION WORKFLOW (GAP-FOCUSED RESEARCH OVER AUDITED SESSIONS)\n\nYou will run a single enhance session that reads audited session outputs and fills gaps using new, audited content. The source sessions may come from any audited skill (research, review, or a prior enhance). Each source contains per-paragraph audit footnotes with Confidence levels (HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW).\n\nInputs (absolute paths provided):\n- TASK_FILE: {{TASK_FILE}}                     # Objectives / target state\n- KB_DIR (optional): {{KB_DIR}}                # Optional KB context (markdown docs)\n- SESSIONS_DIR: {{SESSIONS_DIR}}               # Root directory with prior workflow sessions\n- SESSION_DIR: {{SESSION_DIR}}                 # Current enhance session directory\n- FORMAT_RULES: {{FORMAT_RULES_PATH}}\n- CITATIONS_RULES: {{CITATIONS_RULES_PATH}}    # Typically citations.enhance.md\n- AUDIT_RULES: {{AUDIT_RULES_PATH}}            # Paragraph-level audit rules\n- MANIFEST: {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json      # CLI-provided manifest (authoritative I/O)\n\nData sources (local only):\n- The SOURCES listed in the objective are the audited session files to enhance.\n  Read each source file. These contain text with per-paragraph audit footnotes.\n- MANIFEST is authoritative for the output filename (`outputs.primary`).\n- Optional KB markdown files in KB_DIR for additional context (never as sole evidence).\n\nGoal:\nProduce ONE session-level enhance document, fully audited paragraph-by-paragraph.\nOutput filename is controlled by MANIFEST:\n- Read MANIFEST first.\n- Write the session output to:\n  {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\nDo NOT derive output filenames from the workflow filename or from TASK_FILE.\n\nThis document:\n- May introduce new or corrected content to close gaps in the audited KB.\n- MUST attach an audit footnote to every paragraph according to AUDIT_RULES.\n- MUST keep citations in TOKEN form ([KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]), not [@Key].\n- WILL LATER be consumed by a compile-type workflow (compile-enhance) that converts tokens to [@Key] and generates a bibliography file.\n\nMethod:\n\n1) Load objectives from TASK_FILE\n   - Read TASK_FILE and extract:\n     - Overall objectives and required deliverables.\n     - Explicit focus areas, questions, and constraints.\n   - Treat TASK_FILE as the **target state** you want the session output document to satisfy.\n   - If TASK_FILE contains `## SLOTS` and `## CONSTRAINTS`, treat it as a\n     structured prompt. Parse the slot headings and treat each slot as a\n     separate section in the output. For each slot, follow that slot's\n     instructions including any format directive in the description.\n\n2) Read MANIFEST and audited source sessions\n- Read MANIFEST from {{SESSION_DIR}}/manifest.json to obtain `outputs.primary`.\n- Read each file listed in SOURCES (from the objective). These are audited\n  session documents containing text with per-paragraph audit footnotes.\n- For each paragraph across the source sessions:\n     - Read its existing audit footnote (`^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]`).\n       - If the rationale states that the KB paragraph is incorrect, contradicted by evidence, or contains serious factual errors, or if `Confidence` is LOW → **requires correction**.\n       - If the rationale highlights gaps, mixed evidence, or unresolved doubts, or if `Confidence` is MEDIUM  **requires clarification / additional evidence**.\n       - If the rationale states that the KB paragraph is well supported and `Confidence` is HIGH  factually sound; candidate to preserve, but may still be incomplete w.r.t. objectives.\n   - Cross-check against TASK_FILE objectives:\n     - Identify which objectives are fully covered by HIGH-confidence paragraphs whose rationales indicate strong support.\n     - Identify objectives partially covered (need expansion or detail).\n     - Identify objectives not covered at all.\n\n3) Targeted research for gaps\n   - For each gap (LOW/MEDIUM confidence, rationales indicating contradictions/uncertainty, partially covered objectives, or missing objectives):\n     - Use web search/fetch to gather **authoritative external evidence**.\n       - Prefer DOIs / ARXIV / official standards / institutional reports.\n     - DO NOT rely solely on low-confidence or contradicted/uncertain paragraphs as evidence.\n     - For HIGH-confidence paragraphs, you MAY use their content as KB context, but any new claims must be backed by explicit external citations.\n   - Summarize the relevant evidence for each gap, keeping notes of which sources (WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/KB:) support which claims.\n\n4) Draft new and improved paragraphs (with citations)\n   - For each gap:\n     - Write one or more new or revised paragraphs that:\n       - Correct FALSE statements.\n       - Clarify or strengthen UNCERTAIN or low-confidence claims.\n       - Add missing content for uncovered objectives.\n     - Attach inline TOKEN citations to support each factual claim:\n       - `[WEB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]`, and, when appropriate, `[KB:...]`.\n     - Maintain a professional technical tone and clear structure per FORMAT_RULES.\n   - Preserve TRUE/HIGH paragraphs from source sessions when they already satisfy objectives:\n     - You may copy them verbatim or lightly edit for clarity, but DO NOT change their meaning.\n     - If you expand them, ensure new statements have explicit external citations.\n\n5) Apply per-paragraph audit (AUDIT_RULES)\n   - For **every paragraph** in the session document (preserved, corrected, expanded, or new):\n     - Apply AUDIT_RULES to produce:\n       - `Confidence: HIGH / MEDIUM / LOW` about how well supported the paragraph is by the available evidence.\n       - A `Rationale` summarizing the evidence and reasoning, explicitly stating whether the paragraph is well supported, incorrect, or uncertain and why.\n     - Append the audit footnote immediately after the paragraph in the canonical format:\n       - `^[Confidence: ..., Rationale: ...]`\n   - This step is mandatory: no paragraph may remain without an audit footnote.\n\n6) Post-processing and citation rules\n   - Apply FORMAT_RULES to the full session document:\n     - Professional voice, math notation, heading levels, etc.\n   - Apply CITATIONS_RULES for **session-style** citations:\n     - Normalize all citations to TOKEN form: `[KB:...]`, `[WEB:...]`, `[DOI:...]`, `[ARXIV:...]`, `[ZOTERO:...]`.\n     - You MUST NOT convert citations to `[@Key]` in this workflow.\n\n7) Write session output\n   - Read MANIFEST and use `outputs.primary` as the only valid output filename.\n   - Save/write the full audited session document to:\n     - {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\n   - Confirm in your final message (without printing document contents):\n     - \"Enhanced session document written to {{SESSION_DIR}}/<MANIFEST.outputs.primary>\"\n\nConstraints:\n- Use only local audit outputs and optional KB files as starting context; **all new or corrected claims must be supported by explicit external citations** via web search/fetch.\n- Do NOT perform any compile/consensus logic across multiple enhance sessions; each run is an independent session.\n- Keep citations in TOKEN form; do not emit `[@Key]`.\n- If you include Markdown code blocks in the output, treat them as opaque: do not place citations or audit footnotes inside the block; place any required audit footnote immediately after the closing fence.\n- Do not create any separate bibliography file in this workflow (no `biblatex.bib` here).\n- Do not treat this workflow's output as a client-ready deliverable; it is an intermediate, audited session document to be consumed by `tito compile`.\n\nEnhancement Principles:\n- Contradicted/incorrect claims (per rationale) → Correct with authoritative sources\n- Uncertain or LOW/MEDIUM confidence claims → Resolve with additional evidence\n- Missing objectives → Research and add new content\n- Incomplete coverage → Expand with proper detail\n- All enhancements → Must have citation support\n- Preserve HIGH-confidence content → Only expand if objectives require it\n\nExternal capabilities (if available):\n- Web access (search + fetch): REQUIRED for gap research and fact verification\n- File reading: to read the audited source sessions listed in SOURCES\n- Optional orchestrator memory tool: MAY be used strictly as shared cache/state for repeated web work. Do NOT cite memory as evidence; all new or corrected claims must still be supported by explicit external citations.\n","numLines":125,"startLine":1,"totalLines":125}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01JzkY4Re9iqghs5M4qF9S3K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QM3BVnWUWHUdsVSexVq26o","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13621,"cache_read_input_tokens":16297,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13621,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"249e2e93-5ddb-4b1b-a788-8df9e70b5896","uuid":"4a97fa19-0d1b-4b4e-a660-d5d14edceef4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QM3BVnWUWHUdsVSexVq26o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n4\t\n5\tIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n6\t\n7\tPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n8\t1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n9\t2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n10\t3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n11\t4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n12\t5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n13\t\n14\tQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n15\t- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n16\t- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n17\t- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n18\t- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n19\t\n20\tNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n21\t\n22\tMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n23\t\n24\tInline Math ($...$ format):\n25\t- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n26\t- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n27\t- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n28\t- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n29\t\n30\tDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n31\t- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n32\t- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n33\t- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n34\t- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n35\t- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n36\t- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n37\t\n38\tFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n39\t- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n40\t- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n41\t- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n42\t- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n43\t\n44\tMath Notation Standards:\n45\t- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n46\t- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n47\t- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n48\t- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n49\t- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n50\t\n51\tPROSE STYLE:\n52\t\n53\tProfessional/Academic Voice:\n54\t- Impersonal, objective tone\n55\t- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n56\t- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n57\t- Remove instructional tone\n58\t- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n59\t\n60\tProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n61\t- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n62\t- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n63\t- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n64\t- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n65\t\n66\tProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n67\t- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n68\t- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n69\t- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n70\t\n71\tCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n72\t- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n73\t- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n74\t- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n75\t- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n76\t- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n77\t\n78\tDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n79\t- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n80\t- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n81\t- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n82\t\n83\tCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n84\t\n85\tMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n86\t- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n87\t- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n88\t- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n89\t- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n90\t- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n91\t\n92\tDeduplication:\n93\t- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n94\t- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n95\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n96\t\n97\tCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n98\t- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n99\t- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n100\t- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n101\t\n102\tFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n103\t- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n104\t- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n105\t\n106\tOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\n107\tProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n108\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"249e2e93-5ddb-4b1b-a788-8df9e70b5896","uuid":"3ab4ccf1-539c-4c6c-a3d1-ea15f353f5ed","timestamp":"2026-03-30T21:26:51.552Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/format.session.md","content":"PROFESSIONAL DOCUMENT STYLE FORMATTING\n\nPurpose: Transform technical documents into publication-ready professional format.\n\nIMPORTANT: BEFORE FORMATTING - ANALYZE DOCUMENT STATE\n\nPRE-FORMAT ANALYSIS REQUIREMENTS:\n1. **Check Current Format**: Assess if the document already meets professional clarity and consistency\n2. **Mathematical Notation Check**: Verify if equations use proper LaTeX format ($...$, $$...$$) where applicable\n3. **Citation Format**: Ensure citations follow project token policy or normalized [@key]\n4. **Quality Assessment**: Determine if the document exhibits publication-ready professional characteristics\n5. **De-duplication and Consistency**: Detect duplicated content and potential contradictions; resolve when possible\n\nQUALITY TARGETS (NON-GATING):\n- Clear organization suitable for a professional technical audience\n- Mathematical expressions (if present) in LaTeX format ($...$ or `$$...$$`)\n- Consistent, normalized citations (project tokens or [@key])\n- Professional/academic voice and consistent technical terminology\n\nNote: These targets are mandatory goals to achieve. Always apply all formatting checks regardless of perceived document quality.\n\nMATHEMATICAL EXPRESSIONS:\n\nInline Math ($...$ format):\n- Use for mathematical symbols, variables, and simple expressions within text\n- Examples: $x$, $f(x)$, $n > 100$, $\\alpha = 0.05$, $O(n \\log n)$\n- Use for brief formulas: $E[X] = \\mu$, $\\beta_1 = \\mu_3^2/\\mu_2^3$\n- Single-line expressions that flow with text must be short: $Z = \\gamma + \\delta f((X - \\xi)/\\lambda)$\n\nDisplay Math ($$...$$ format):\n- Use for standalone equations that deserve emphasis or are complex\n- Multi-line expressions, definitions, theorems\n- Integration/summation with multiple terms: $$H[f] = -\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\log f(x) dx$$\n- Matrix expressions, optimization problems\n- Equations with fractions, large operators, or multiple levels: $$\\kappa = \\frac{\\beta_1(\\beta_2+3)^2}{4(4\\beta_2-3\\beta_1)(2\\beta_2-3\\beta_1-6)}$$\n- Piecewise functions, case definitions\n\nFORBIDDEN Math Formats:\n- Never use \\(...\\) or \\[...\\]\n- No Unicode symbols in equations (β, α, ∑, ∫)\n- No inconsistent notation (use $\\lambda$ throughout, not switching to $l$)\n- No equations in code blocks unless showing actual code\n\nMath Notation Standards:\n- Greek letters: $\\alpha, \\beta, \\gamma, \\delta, \\lambda, \\mu, \\sigma, \\theta, \\phi, \\psi, \\omega$\n- Operators: $\\sum, \\prod, \\int, \\partial, \\nabla, \\infty$\n- Relations: $\\leq, \\geq, \\neq, \\approx, \\equiv, \\in, \\subset$\n- Functions: $\\log, \\ln, \\sin, \\cos, \\exp, \\sqrt{}, \\frac{}{}$\n- Define all symbols clearly on first use\n\nPROSE STYLE:\n\nProfessional/Academic Voice:\n- Impersonal, objective tone\n- Present tense for established facts, past tense for specific studies\n- Avoid promotional/commercial language\n- Remove instructional tone\n- Convert to neutral academic phrasing\n\nProhibited rhetorical modes (applies to all workflows)\n- Instructional language: no imperatives, reader directions, or authorial roadmaps (e.g., addressing what the reader should do or what the text \"will\" do next)\n- Motivational/promotional claims: no value‑laden benefits to an audience, no capability or marketing assertions about the work; avoid audience‑targeted promises\n- Second‑person address: do not address the reader directly\n- Rewrite such expressions as descriptive, evidence‑based statements in an impersonal, declarative form\n\nProblematic Language Patterns (convert to academic tone):\n- \"enables\" -> \"permits\", \"allows\", \"makes possible\"\n- \"powerful\"-> \"effective\", \"capable\"\n- \"cutting-edge\" → \"recent\", \"current\", \"modern\"\n\nCHARACTER FORMATTING:\n- For ENGLISH outputs: ASCII text only (no accented letters or non-ASCII punctuation)\n- For non-English outputs: allow the minimal extended Latin characters required by the target language (e.g., á, é, ñ) but keep punctuation ASCII\n- Straight quotes (\", ') not smart quotes (\", \", ', ')\n- Standard hyphens (-) not em-dashes (—)\n- No special Unicode symbols outside LaTeX math\n\nDE-DUPLICATION AND CONSISTENCY CHECKS:\n- Identify and remove duplicated sentences, sections, tables, or figures.\n- Detect contradictory statements; reconcile using KB and external citations; if unresolved, keep the more reliable/cited version.\n- Ensure units, symbols, and terminology are consistent throughout.\n\nCITATIONS — Session workflow policy (RESEARCH/REVIEW)\n\nMANDATORY REQUIREMENT:\n- PRESERVE all token citations in the final document exactly as they appear\n- Supported tokens: [KB:filename.md], [WEB:https://...], [DOI:...], [ARXIV:...]\n- Do NOT convert tokens to [@key] format\n- Do NOT remove any citation tokens\n- Reasoning: Token citations are required for downstream audit verification and traceability\n\nDeduplication:\n- Ensure a single consistent citation per claim\n- If multiple tokens cite the same source, keep one representative token\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support\n\nCODE BLOCKS (MARKDOWN/PANDOC/QUARTO)\n- Treat fenced code blocks (backticks or tildes; length >= 3, including 4+; may include Quarto language headers like `{r}` on the opening fence) and indented code blocks as **opaque**.\n- Do NOT modify content inside code blocks during formatting.\n- Do NOT insert citations or audit footnotes inside code blocks; place any required metadata outside the block (typically immediately after the closing fence).\n\nFILESYSTEM AND LOG ACCESS CONSTRAINTS:\n- NEVER read or use orchestration log files as input to any workflow. In particular, you MUST NOT read files such as `agent.out`, `RUN_METADATA.txt`, or other raw transcripts produced by the execution layer; they exist only for debugging on disk and must not be sent back into Claude-flow or used as evidence.\n- When using filesystem tools (Read/Glob/Bash/find), skip any entries named exactly `current` or obvious symlinks to `*/sessions/run-*`; treat them as internal implementation details, not as documents or data sources.\n\nOUTPUT REQUIREMENT:\nProduce a publication-ready professional document requiring no additional formatting.\n","numLines":108,"startLine":1,"totalLines":108}}}
