{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017z1ewKa5N8E7uZPuUhLgD4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCITATION RULES — GAP-FOCUSED TOKEN DOCUMENTS (ENHANCE)\n2\t\n3\tGoal\n4\t- Define how enhance session outputs represent and use citations when operating over `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n5\t- Ensure that no content previously audited as FALSE is promoted as evidence, and that all new/corrected claims are backed by explicit external sources.\n6\t\n7\tCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n8\t- Enhance sessions use TOKEN citations; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n9\t- Supported tokens:\n10\t  - Knowledge Base / compiled review documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (including `<id>.compile.review.md` and any underlying KB `.md` files when they act as context).\n11\t  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n12\t  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n13\t  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n14\t  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n15\t- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in enhance workflows.\n16\t- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n17\t\n18\tPlacement Rules\n19\t- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n20\t- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n21\t  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n22\t  - `... [KB:hazard.compile.review.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n23\t- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented). If you need to cite evidence about code, cite in prose outside the code block.\n24\t- Make clear, across the document, which claims are grounded in the audited KB and which in external sources.\n25\t\n26\tEvidence Policy (ENHANCE-SPECIFIC)\n27\t- `<id>.compile.review.md` provides the starting point and context for enhance sessions, but it is **not** sufficient on its own as supporting evidence.\n28\t- Paragraphs whose audit Rationales (or legacy `Verdict:` labels) indicate that the KB paragraph is **factually wrong or contradicted by evidence** MUST NOT be treated as positive evidence for any claim.\n29\t- Paragraphs whose Rationales describe uncertainty or mixed/insufficient evidence, or whose `Confidence` is LOW, may be used only to frame uncertainty, never as sole support for a factual statement.\n30\t- For sections whose audit Rationales state that the KB paragraph is well supported and whose `Confidence` is HIGH (including any legacy `Verdict: TRUE` labels):\n31\t-  - You may treat the audited text as KB-style context (`[KB:...]`).\n32\t-  - Any **new or expanded** claims beyond what was already audited as well supported with HIGH confidence MUST be backed by explicit external citations `[WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`.\n33\t- For gaps (FALSE, UNCERTAIN, LOW confidence, missing or partially covered objectives):\n34\t  - All corrective or gap-filling paragraphs MUST include external citations.\n35\t  - When several external sources disagree, cite the most authoritative ones and reflect the remaining uncertainty in the audit footnote.\n36\t\n37\tCoverage Rules\n38\t- For every major enhancement area (objective or gap), ensure that at least one external citation is present in the paragraphs that close that gap.\n39\t- Keep `[KB:...]` tokens for:\n40\t  - Referring back to specific reviewed sections in `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n41\t  - Marking when you reuse or paraphrase TRUE/HIGH content.\n42\t- Do **not** rely solely on `[KB:...]` in new paragraphs; at least one external token should appear wherever you introduce new factual material.\n43\t\n44\tDeduplication\n45\t- Within a paragraph, avoid redundant repetition of the same token unless it improves clarity.\n46\t- Across the document:\n47\t  - Reuse the same token when referring to the same underlying source.\n48\t  - Prefer a small number of strong sources over many weak or redundant WEB links.\n49\t\n50\tInteraction with Audit Rules\n51\t- Every paragraph in `<id>.enhance.md` will receive a footnote per `rules/audit.md`.\n52\t- When writing the audit Rationale, refer back to the same tokens you used in the paragraph body:\n53\t-  - Example: \"Supported by `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]` and `[WEB:https://...]`; previous KB paragraph was judged incorrect due to outdated values.\"\n54\t- If evidence remains genuinely insufficient, explain this clearly in the Rationale and choose a cautious `Confidence` level instead of over-stating certainty.\n55\t\n56\tOutput Requirements\n57\t- Enhance session outputs MUST:\n58\t  - Keep all citations in TOKEN form only.\n59\t  - Use `[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]` consistently according to this policy.\n60\t  - Never treat previously FALSE-audited content as standalone evidence.\n61\t  - Provide enough external citations that compile-enhance can later map tokens to `[@Key]` and generate a complete `biblatex.bib`.\n62\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"249e2e93-5ddb-4b1b-a788-8df9e70b5896","uuid":"afc88d16-8e33-4977-adc0-4bc43eb90d0d","timestamp":"2026-03-30T21:26:51.973Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/usr/local/libexec/tito/rules/citations.enhance.md","content":"CITATION RULES — GAP-FOCUSED TOKEN DOCUMENTS (ENHANCE)\n\nGoal\n- Define how enhance session outputs represent and use citations when operating over `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n- Ensure that no content previously audited as FALSE is promoted as evidence, and that all new/corrected claims are backed by explicit external sources.\n\nCitation Representation (MANDATORY)\n- Enhance sessions use TOKEN citations; do NOT use [@Key] here.\n- Supported tokens:\n  - Knowledge Base / compiled review documents: `[KB:filename.md]` (including `<id>.compile.review.md` and any underlying KB `.md` files when they act as context).\n  - External web sources: `[WEB:https://full-url]`.\n  - ArXiv preprints: `[ARXIV:paper-id]`.\n  - DOI-identified works: `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n  - Optional Zotero items: `[ZOTERO:item_key]` when a Zotero MCP is used.\n- Do **not** convert token citations to [@Key] in enhance workflows.\n- Do **not** remove any valid citation tokens that appear in the draft.\n\nPlacement Rules\n- Place citations immediately after the sentence they support.\n- Multiple citations are allowed, e.g.:\n  - `... [KB:doc1.md][WEB:https://...]`.\n  - `... [KB:hazard.compile.review.md][DOI:10.0000/xyz123]`.\n- Never place citation tokens inside Markdown code blocks (fenced or indented). If you need to cite evidence about code, cite in prose outside the code block.\n- Make clear, across the document, which claims are grounded in the audited KB and which in external sources.\n\nEvidence Policy (ENHANCE-SPECIFIC)\n- `<id>.compile.review.md` provides the starting point and context for enhance sessions, but it is **not** sufficient on its own as supporting evidence.\n- Paragraphs whose audit Rationales (or legacy `Verdict:` labels) indicate that the KB paragraph is **factually wrong or contradicted by evidence** MUST NOT be treated as positive evidence for any claim.\n- Paragraphs whose Rationales describe uncertainty or mixed/insufficient evidence, or whose `Confidence` is LOW, may be used only to frame uncertainty, never as sole support for a factual statement.\n- For sections whose audit Rationales state that the KB paragraph is well supported and whose `Confidence` is HIGH (including any legacy `Verdict: TRUE` labels):\n-  - You may treat the audited text as KB-style context (`[KB:...]`).\n-  - Any **new or expanded** claims beyond what was already audited as well supported with HIGH confidence MUST be backed by explicit external citations `[WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]`.\n- For gaps (FALSE, UNCERTAIN, LOW confidence, missing or partially covered objectives):\n  - All corrective or gap-filling paragraphs MUST include external citations.\n  - When several external sources disagree, cite the most authoritative ones and reflect the remaining uncertainty in the audit footnote.\n\nCoverage Rules\n- For every major enhancement area (objective or gap), ensure that at least one external citation is present in the paragraphs that close that gap.\n- Keep `[KB:...]` tokens for:\n  - Referring back to specific reviewed sections in `<id>.compile.review.md`.\n  - Marking when you reuse or paraphrase TRUE/HIGH content.\n- Do **not** rely solely on `[KB:...]` in new paragraphs; at least one external token should appear wherever you introduce new factual material.\n\nDeduplication\n- Within a paragraph, avoid redundant repetition of the same token unless it improves clarity.\n- Across the document:\n  - Reuse the same token when referring to the same underlying source.\n  - Prefer a small number of strong sources over many weak or redundant WEB links.\n\nInteraction with Audit Rules\n- Every paragraph in `<id>.enhance.md` will receive a footnote per `rules/audit.md`.\n- When writing the audit Rationale, refer back to the same tokens you used in the paragraph body:\n-  - Example: \"Supported by `[DOI:10.0000/xyz123]` and `[WEB:https://...]`; previous KB paragraph was judged incorrect due to outdated values.\"\n- If evidence remains genuinely insufficient, explain this clearly in the Rationale and choose a cautious `Confidence` level instead of over-stating certainty.\n\nOutput Requirements\n- Enhance session outputs MUST:\n  - Keep all citations in TOKEN form only.\n  - Use `[KB:/WEB:/DOI:/ARXIV:/ZOTERO:]` consistently according to this policy.\n  - Never treat previously FALSE-audited content as standalone evidence.\n  - Provide enough external citations that compile-enhance can later map tokens to `[@Key]` and generate a complete `biblatex.bib`.\n","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":62}}}
